# RP-3.11: Polymorphic and Metamorphic Binaries

## Abstract

Modern computer systems face severe threats from attackers. These attackers reuse existing code structures to gain unauthorized control. This document presents a theoretical architecture for polymorphic and metamorphic binaries. The architecture creates a dynamic software environment. It changes the assembly code and the memory layout during operation. This mechanism functions as a digital immune system. It invalidates the prior knowledge of an attacker. At the same time, the system maintains reproducible builds on the storage disk. This separation ensures supply-chain trust while it maximizes security.

## 1. Introduction

Static binary files present a large attack surface. If an attacker finds a weakness on one system, they can use the same exploit on all identical systems. This condition allows mass exploitation. 

A polymorphic and metamorphic binary system changes its own structure. It alters registers, instruction sequences, and memory locations at runtime. The logical result of the software remains identical. However, the internal layout changes continuously or at specific load times. This change makes it impossible for attackers to find a stable target.

A conflict exists between dynamic runtime software and deterministic reproducible builds. Reproducible builds require that the same source code always produces the exact same bytes on the storage disk. This determinism is necessary for supply-chain audits. To resolve this conflict, the architecture applies changes only in the computer memory. The build process creates a deterministic hash on the disk. The software changes its code only when the operating system loads it. This separation ensures that the immune system and the verifiable supply chain operate together without interference.

## 2. Problem Statement

The need for runtime changes comes from specific threats. Mass exploit kits rely on a single gadget layout that fits all systems. Memory exploits use uniform memory layouts to scale their attacks. Targeted implants depend on stable code offsets over the lifetime of a process. A shared cloud image means that a single exploit can break an entire fleet of servers. 

Address Space Layout Randomization provides some protection. However, it is coarse, and attackers can sometimes read the memory layout. Continuous changes of the memory layout and the executable code increase the engineering cost for the attacker significantly.

Different stakeholders have different needs in this ecosystem. The defender needs a different attack surface across systems and time. The supply-chain auditor requires rebuilds that are fully identical on the disk. The debugger needs symbols and reproducible failures to find errors. The performance owner requires a limit on the computational cost of the changes. The adversary attempts to find information leaks to discover the memory layout. The adversary also attacks the modification engine directly.

## 3. Related Work

Previous research shows various methods for software diversity. Some compilers generate software diversity at compile time. A multicompiler can randomize layouts for global variables, the stack, functions, and virtual tables. Obfuscation tools transform control flow and change instructions. These tools trade performance for security through obscurity. Profile-guided software diversity uses performance data to optimize the changes. Systems can also run multiple changed instances of a program and cross-check their behavior.

Load-time and runtime changes also exist. Address Space Layout Randomization provides coarse base randomization. Fine-grained randomization shuffles functions and basic blocks. Continuous systems change the layout during operation. However, they have a high engineering cost.

Reproducible builds provide identical files. Diverse double-compilation uses two compilers to defend against trust attacks. Expert surveys show that reproducibility has high utility for supply-chain security. The key insight is that runtime changes and disk reproducibility both provide security. However, they operate at different layers.

## 4. Architecture and Methodology

The architecture uses a two-phase model. In the first phase, a deterministic compiler processes the source code. This compiler produces a static file on the disk. This file has a stable, verifiable hash. In the second phase, a loader or an in-memory modification engine reads the file. The engine creates a runtime instance in the memory. This instance changes in time and space.

The system defines different levels of transformation. Level zero provides only basic layout randomization. Level one shuffles functions from a random seed at load time. Level two reorders basic blocks or renames registers during memory compilation. Level three continuously changes the code at regular time intervals. The architecture focuses on memory-based transformations. It does not use on-disk diversification.

The transformations require a source of entropy. The modification engine uses a secure capability to access random seeds. The semantic identity of the software must remain intact. A transformation is valid only if the behavior of the modified code is strictly identical to the original code for all inputs. Software changes alter only the undefined layouts that attackers use for exploits.

## 5. Conflict Resolution

The architecture must resolve several internal conflicts. The primary conflict is between continuous changes and deterministic builds. The strict separation of disk determinism and memory diversification resolves this problem.

A second conflict exists between code modifications and control flow integrity. Self-modifying code can invalidate security signatures. The system must create new signatures for the code after a transformation. Alternatively, it must restrict changes to data structures when control flow integrity is active.

A third conflict involves performance. Continuous changes of large code segments require significant computational time. The architecture reduces this by transforming code at load time or during rare epochs. It avoids continuous changes every few milliseconds unless the process is highly sensitive.

A fourth conflict affects debugging. Debugging tools need a stable map of the code. The system provides map files that correlate the randomized memory locations back to the original source code. The user can also disable the modification engine during explicit debugging sessions.

## 6. Conclusion

Polymorphic and metamorphic binaries provide a robust defense against memory corruption exploits and code-reuse attacks. The architecture transforms the memory layout and the instruction sequences at load time or during runtime. It prevents attackers from relying on static offsets. The strict separation between the deterministic disk file and the dynamic memory instance ensures that supply-chain verification remains intact. This design creates a software immune system. This system significantly increases the cost of exploitation while it preserves the trust anchor of reproducible builds.

